00:09:11.519 --> 00:09:14.959
exercise in, like playing some pickle

00:09:12.959 --> 00:09:16.800
ball for instance, gets you 90% of the

00:09:14.959 --> 00:09:18.320
way there. And the guy doing this simple

00:09:16.800 --> 00:09:20.640
thing consistently is going to

00:09:18.320 --> 00:09:22.560
absolutely crush the guy designing the

00:09:20.640 --> 00:09:24.480
perfect program that he never actually

00:09:22.560 --> 00:09:26.160
starts or he does it but he doesn't do

00:09:24.480 --> 00:09:28.080
it consistently. So I've watched people

00:09:26.160 --> 00:09:30.000
spend weeks building this perfect

00:09:28.080 --> 00:09:32.320
elaborate AI automation to save

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:34.240
themselves 10 minutes a week. That math

00:09:32.320 --> 00:09:36.000
just does not work. That is not being

00:09:34.240 --> 00:09:37.920
smart. That's just expensive

00:09:36.000 --> 00:09:39.680
procrastination. And the MIT report

00:09:37.920 --> 00:09:41.760
found that more than half of a company's

00:09:39.680 --> 00:09:43.760
AI budgets were going into flashy

00:09:41.760 --> 00:09:46.399
exciting stuff like sales and marketing

00:09:43.760 --> 00:09:48.160
tools. But the biggest actual return was

00:09:46.399 --> 00:09:50.000
actually in the boring back office

00:09:48.160 --> 00:09:51.600
automation that nobody talked about,

00:09:50.000 --> 00:09:54.000
right? So people were chasing the shiny

00:09:51.600 --> 00:09:56.320
20% and completely ignoring the simple

00:09:54.000 --> 00:09:57.920
80% that actually paid off. So here's

00:09:56.320 --> 00:10:00.160
the move. Before you build anything with

00:09:57.920 --> 00:10:01.920
AI, ask yourself one question. What is

00:10:00.160 --> 00:10:03.120
the laziest and simplest version of this

00:10:01.920 --> 00:10:05.760
that's still going to get me most of the

00:10:03.120 --> 00:10:07.760
result. Then build that. Ship it. If you

00:10:05.760 --> 00:10:09.279
genuinely need the fancy version later,

00:10:07.760 --> 00:10:11.519
you can add it. But nine times out of

00:10:09.279 --> 00:10:13.040
10, you won't need to. So get the 80,

00:10:11.519 --> 00:10:14.720
then skip the grind for the last five.

00:10:13.040 --> 00:10:17.200
All right. Rule number five, and this

00:10:14.720 --> 00:10:19.200
might be the real cheat code, is speed.

00:10:17.200 --> 00:10:20.399
The speed at which you implement is more

00:10:19.200 --> 00:10:22.800
important than getting it perfect

00:10:20.399 --> 00:10:24.480
because your first version is never

00:10:22.800 --> 00:10:26.079
going to be perfect anyways, right?

00:10:24.480 --> 00:10:27.760
Whatever you build first is going to be

00:10:26.079 --> 00:10:29.600
kind of bad. And that's fine. That is

00:10:27.760 --> 00:10:31.760
normal. The winners don't avoid that.

00:10:29.600 --> 00:10:34.000
They race straight through it. They ship

00:10:31.760 --> 00:10:36.320
the ugly version fast, see what's

00:10:34.000 --> 00:10:37.760
broken, and then fix it live as they go

00:10:36.320 --> 00:10:40.079
along. And this is true with companies

00:10:37.760 --> 00:10:42.240
as well. Airbnb started off as a crappy

00:10:40.079 --> 00:10:44.399
web form. YouTube started off as a video

00:10:42.240 --> 00:10:45.920
dating site. And Slack started off as a

00:10:44.399 --> 00:10:47.440
failed video game, right? It's kind of

00:10:45.920 --> 00:10:49.279
like learning how to ride a bike. You

00:10:47.440 --> 00:10:50.959
could read every book in the world about

00:10:49.279 --> 00:10:52.480
riding bikes. You could watch all the

00:10:50.959 --> 00:10:54.320
documentaries in the world about how to

00:10:52.480 --> 00:10:56.160
ride a bike. You could hire whoever won

00:10:54.320 --> 00:10:57.839
the tour to France most recently to

00:10:56.160 --> 00:11:00.399
teach you how to ride a bike. But until

00:10:57.839 --> 00:11:02.480
you actually jump on the bike and start

00:11:00.399 --> 00:11:03.920
trying to ride it, you are not going to

00:11:02.480 --> 00:11:05.519
know how to ride a bike. It doesn't

00:11:03.920 --> 00:11:06.800
matter if you try to study it for 20

00:11:05.519 --> 00:11:08.240
years, right? So getting started as

00:11:06.800 --> 00:11:10.720
quickly as possible and shipping a

00:11:08.240 --> 00:11:13.920
minimum viable product is the way to go

00:11:10.720 --> 00:11:15.519
and speed is king. Okay, speed is the

00:11:13.920 --> 00:11:17.360
most important thing. The speed of

00:11:15.519 --> 00:11:19.519
implementation is pretty much directly

00:11:17.360 --> 00:11:21.760
proportional to how successful you are

00:11:19.519 --> 00:11:23.760
going to be in life and with AI as well.

00:11:21.760 --> 00:11:26.000
So the reps are where the growth is not

00:11:23.760 --> 00:11:27.920
the planning but the doing. So stop

00:11:26.000 --> 00:11:30.000
polishing, stop worrying about whether

00:11:27.920 --> 00:11:32.000
the UI is pretty, stop worrying about

00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:33.360
all that other stuff. Just ship it. Now

00:11:32.000 --> 00:11:35.120
you might be thinking but what if it's

00:11:33.360 --> 00:11:37.040
not good enough yet? It's not and it

00:11:35.120 --> 00:11:39.279
never will be with version one. You make

00:11:37.040 --> 00:11:41.040
it good by shipping it and fixing it,

00:11:39.279 --> 00:11:43.440
not by hiding it. And this is exactly

00:11:41.040 --> 00:11:45.040
how I operate, right? When I use AI for

00:11:43.440 --> 00:11:46.800
something new, my first version is

00:11:45.040 --> 00:11:48.959
almost always rough. But I get it out

00:11:46.800 --> 00:11:50.800
the door fast. I see what works and then

00:11:48.959 --> 00:11:52.480
I improve it from there. Right? Ship a

00:11:50.800 --> 00:11:54.160
minimum viable product as fast as you

00:11:52.480 --> 00:11:55.760
can and then stress test it and then

00:11:54.160 --> 00:11:57.279
tweak it and modify it from there. And

00:11:55.760 --> 00:11:59.120
remember those startups from MIT that

00:11:57.279 --> 00:12:01.120
went from 0 to 20 million? Well, they

00:11:59.120 --> 00:12:02.640
did not spend a year polishing in the

00:12:01.120 --> 00:12:04.480
lab. They shipped, they got real

00:12:02.640 --> 00:12:06.079
feedback, and they iterated. So, here's

00:12:04.480 --> 00:12:08.639
how to start this today. Pick that one

00:12:06.079 --> 00:12:10.399
task. Give Claude one solid attempt at

00:12:08.639 --> 00:12:12.160
it, and then actually use the results,

00:12:10.399 --> 00:12:13.920
even if it's only 80% there. And don't

00:12:12.160 --> 00:12:15.920
